### 9.6 Systematic Uncertainty

The systematic uncertainties are shown in Figure 9.2 and given in Table 9.11. The major systematic uncertainty is due the uncertainty on the jet energy scale (JES). Because the \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) dependence is steeply decreasing, the uncertainty of the cross section is very sensitive to systematic uncertainty of the JES. For example, 5% of the JES uncertainty results in 30% of the cross section uncertainty at 20 GeV and 60% at 40 GeV.

#### Jet Energy Scale (Tracks)

The track portion of the jet energy has 5.6 % of systematic uncertainty [40, 41]. To evaluate the effect of this uncertainty on the inclusive jet cross section, the cross section is reevaluated with the 5.6 % variation of the track portion of the jet energy.

The effect of this variation is only a shift of the track portion of the jet energy and does not include a change of the trigger rates. Therefore, this variation does not change the total jet cross section as much as the neutral energy variation does. As the track portion of the jet energy is raised (lowered), the jet cross section decreases (increases) in the low-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) range and increases (decreases) in the high-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) range. The cross section is insensitive to the variation at around \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) 20GeV. The uncertainty is large in the high-\(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) range because the neutral energy ratio is small and the \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) dependence is steep at the detector level.

